  "docTimeline1990sTitle": "A Swedish invention",
  "docTimeline1990sText": "The concept is born in Sweden: a VHF system where every ship announces itself so that others \"see and be seen\", even in fog and behind islands. It is presented to the IMO and becomes the seed of AIS.",
  "docTimeline1998Title": "Standardisation begins",
  "docTimeline1998Text": "The ITU and IEC start turning the concept into a radio standard with precise bit-level formats, based on TDMA over two VHF channels.",
  "docTimeline2001Title": "ITU-R M.1371 published",
  "docTimeline2001Text": "Recommendation ITU-R M.1371 \"Technical characteristics for a universal shipborne automatic identification system\" defines the 27 message types and their bit layout.",
  "docTimeline2002Title": "SOLAS mandate",
  "docTimeline2002Text": "The IMO makes AIS mandatory for all international vessels over 300 gross tons and all passenger ships — roughly 100,000 vessels. AIS becomes a standard anti-collision aid alongside radar.",
  "docTimeline2006Title": "Class B arrives",
  "docTimeline2006Text": "The Class B standard is published, opening the door to cheap, simpler transponders. The same year, the TacSat-2 satellite becomes the first to capture AIS signals from space (S-AIS).",
  "docTimeline2008_2015Title": "Satellite constellations",
  "docTimeline2008_2015Text": "exactEarth, ORBCOMM, Spire and others deploy AIS receivers in low-Earth orbit, extending coverage far beyond the VHF horizon and enabling near-global vessel tracking.",
  "docTimeline2010Title": "AIS-SART in GMDSS",
  "docTimeline2010Text": "The AIS search-and-rescue transmitter (AIS-SART, IEC 61097-14) joins the Global Maritime Distress and Safety System, letting lifeboats broadcast distress positions over AIS.",
  "docTimeline2014Title": "Fisheries & inland fleets",
  "docTimeline2014Text": "European rules require Class A AIS on all EU fishing vessels over 15 m; inland-waterways AIS is widely deployed on European rivers.",
  "docTimeline2021Title": "1.6 million ships",
  "docTimeline2021Text": "More than 1.6 million vessels are fitted with AIS, feeding terrestrial and satellite networks that power ship tracking, fisheries control and maritime security worldwide.",
  "docTimelineVdesTitle": "VDES — the successor",
  "docTimelineVdesText": "The VHF Data Exchange System (ITU-R M.2092) is being rolled out to relieve congested areas, adding far more bandwidth and secure e-navigation services.",

