  "docOverviewTitle": "What is AIS?",
  "docOverviewIntro": "The Automatic Identification System (AIS) is a tracking system used on ships and by vessel traffic services (VTS). Every equipped vessel continuously broadcasts its identity, position, course and speed over VHF radio, so that every other ship and shore station in range can \"see\" it — the concept of \"see and be seen\".",
  "docOverviewRadar": "AIS does not replace marine radar. Radar independently detects any object, but tells you little about who it is. AIS tells you exactly who, where and where they are going — but it trusts what the sender declares. The two systems complement each other.",
  "docOverviewAdsBTitle": "Think of it as the maritime ADS-B",
  "docOverviewAdsBText": "Just as ADS-B lets aircraft announce themselves to air traffic control, AIS lets ships announce themselves to each other and to shore. Ships view surrounding traffic on a chartplotter or on a radar-like display; port authorities monitor movements and fisheries.",
  "docOverviewTransponder": "What a transponder broadcasts",
  "docOverviewBullet1": "Unique identity: a 9-digit MMSI number (whose first three digits identify the issuing country).",
  "docOverviewBullet2": "Dynamic data: position, speed over ground (SOG), course over ground (COG), true heading, rate of turn, navigation status.",
  "docOverviewBullet3": "Static & voyage data: name, call sign, IMO number, ship type, dimensions, draught, destination, ETA.",
  "docOverviewBullet4": "Safety and binary messages: distress texts, weather reports, network commands.",
  "docOverviewWho": "Who must carry it",
  "docOverviewImo": "The IMO (SOLAS convention) mandates AIS on international vessels over 300 gross tons and on all passenger ships. Regional rules extend this to fishing fleets, inland waterways and increasingly to recreational craft via low-cost Class B transponders.",
  "docOverviewLimits": "Limits at a glance",
  "docOverviewLimit1": "Range is roughly line of sight: about 10-20 nautical miles for ship-to-ship, more from coast stations and satellites.",
  "docOverviewLimit2": "AIS has no authentication: anyone can broadcast any identity (spoofing) or jam the channel.",
  "docOverviewLimit3": "Accuracy depends on the sender's GNSS fix and on the honesty of the data it declares.",

  "docHistoryIntro": "AIS grew from a Swedish idea into a worldwide mandatory safety system. Tap any milestone on the timeline for details.",
  "docHistoryStandards": "The governing standards",
  "docHistoryStd1": "ITU-R M.1371 — Technical characteristics for a universal shipborne AIS (defines the 27 message types and their bit layout).",
  "docHistoryStd2": "IALA guidelines — clarifications and implementation guidance.",
  "docHistoryStd3": "IEC 61162 / 62287 — the NMEA sentence framing and Class B/CSTDMA requirements.",
  "docHistoryStd4": "IEC 61097-14 — the AIS-SART distress transmitter.",

  "docHowIntro": "AIS is a VHF radio system. Each transponder listens to the traffic around it and transmits its own reports in reserved time slots, avoiding collisions with the other ships in range.",
  "docHowRadioLink": "The radio link",
  "docHowRadioLink1": "Two dedicated VHF channels: AIS 1 at 161.975 MHz (87B) and AIS 2 at 162.025 MHz (88B).",
  "docHowRadioLink2": "Digital narrow-band FM, at 9 600 bits per second.",
  "docHowRadioLink3": "Messages are organised into TDMA frames of 2250 time slots (1 minute).",
  "docHowSlots": "How slots are shared",
  "docHowSotdma": "Class A transponders use SOTDMA (Self-Organizing Time Division Multiple Access): each unit reserves a repeating slot and re-reserves when the picture changes, so ships continuously coordinate without a central controller.",
  "docHowCstdma": "Class B transponders use the simpler CSTDMA (Carrier Sense TDMA): they listen for a free slot and grab it, which is why Class B reports are less frequent and can be lost in very dense traffic.",
  "docHowRates": "Reporting rates",
  "docHowRates1": "Class A position report (type 1): every 2-10 seconds while underway, every 3 minutes at anchor.",
  "docHowRates2": "Static & voyage data (type 5): every 6 minutes.",
  "docHowRates3": "Class B position (type 18): roughly every 30 seconds.",
  "docHowRates4": "Aid to navigation (type 21): every 3 minutes.",
  "docHowTerrestrial": "Terrestrial and satellite",
  "docHowTerrestrialText": "On the surface, AIS range is limited by the VHF horizon (T-AIS). Since the mid-2000s, satellites in low-Earth orbit (S-AIS) receive the same signals, giving near-global coverage — satellites augment rather than replace the terrestrial network.",

  "docRadioIntro": "Beneath the messages lies a small, efficient radio system. AIS transmits at 9 600 bits per second on two VHF channels, using Gaussian minimum-shift keying (GMSK) and HDLC-style framing.",
  "docRadioPhysical": "The physical link",
  "docRadioPhysical1": "AIS 1 at 161.975 MHz and AIS 2 at 162.025 MHz (VHF channels 87B and 88B).",
  "docRadioPhysical2": "GMSK modulation at 9 600 baud — narrow enough to fit the maritime VHF band.",
  "docRadioPhysical3": "HDLC framing with bit stuffing, and NRZI line coding, inherited from the packet-radio world.",
  "docRadioFrames": "TDMA frames and slots",
  "docRadioFrames1": "Each channel is split into frames of exactly 1 minute, divided into 2 250 time slots of ~26.7 ms each.",
  "docRadioFrames2": "A slot carries one AIS message (256 bits with ramp-up/down and guard time).",
  "docRadioFrames3": "Stations reuse the same slots every frame so they broadcast periodically without colliding.",
  "docRadioCode": "2250 slots/frame · 1 frame = 60 s · slot ≈ 26.7 ms · 9600 bit/s",
  "docRadioSotdma": "SOTDMA — how Class A self-organises",
  "docRadioSotdmaText": "Each Class A transponder listens to the slots around it, picks a free one and announces in its radio-status field when it will transmit next. Stations continuously re-reserve as the traffic picture changes, so no central coordinator is needed.",
  "docRadioCstdma": "CSTDMA — how Class B joins in",
  "docRadioCstdmaText": "Class B units are simpler: they listen for a slot that is currently free and transmit once in it. This is cheaper, but Class B reports can be lost in very dense traffic where a slot is always busy.",
  "docRadioVdes": "VDES — the future",
  "docRadioVdesText": "The VHF Data Exchange System (ITU-R M.2092) is rolling out to relieve congested waters: it adds new frequencies, far more bandwidth and secure two-way data for e-navigation, alongside the existing AIS service.",

  "docClassesIntro": "AIS hardware comes in different classes and roles. The two you will meet most often are the full Class A transponder and the cheap Class B unit.",
  "docClassesComparison": "Class A vs Class B",
  "docClassesReceivers": "Receivers and transponders",
  "docClassesReceiversText": "Transponders both receive and transmit. Many shore stations and hobbyists run receivers only, so they can watch traffic without appearing on it.",
  "docClassesAton": "Aids to navigation",
  "docClassesAtonText": "AtoN stations (type 21) broadcast buoys, beacons and lighthouses. They can also transmit a virtual aid — a marker that exists only on charts, useful to warn of a new hazard.",
  "docClassesDistress": "Distress & safety devices",
  "docClassesDistressIntro": "Beyond regular ships, AIS carries distress transmitters that every receiver should be able to spot:",
  "docClassesSartNote": "A SART in action also sets navigation status 14 (\"AIS-SART active\") on its position report.",

  "docShipTypesIntro": "Type 5 and 24 static messages carry an 8-bit ship-type code (0-99) that describes what the vessel is — cargo, tanker, fishing boat, pleasure craft and so on. The full table is shown below.",
  "docShipTypesCategories": "Categories at a glance",
  "docVesselCatRow": "{range} — {label}",

  "docFieldNotesTitle": "Field notes & real-world quirks",
  "docFieldNotesIntro": "Real AIS traffic does not always match the theory. Knowing these quirks helps you trust what the decoder shows you — and what it rejects.",

  "docGlossaryIntro": "A searchable dictionary of the acronyms and terms used throughout this guide and by the AIS community.",

  "docCheatSheetIntro": "The essential numbers and codes at a glance — frequencies, reporting rates, status codes and formats.",

  "docMmsiIntro": "The Maritime Mobile Service Identity (MMSI) is a unique 9-digit number identifying a ship's radio equipment, like a phone number for the vessel. Its first three digits are the MID — the Maritime Identification Digits that identify the country that issued it.",
  "docMmsiFormats": "Number formats",
  "docMmsiFmtRow": "{format} — {label}",
  "docMmsiLookupHeading": "Look up an MMSI",
  "docMmsiLookupHint": "Enter a 9-digit MMSI below to see its class and the country of the issuing authority.",
  "docMmsiMidHeading": "Country codes (MID)",
  "docMmsiMidText": "The full MID table is bundled with KikAis and used everywhere an MMSI is displayed.",

  "docMessagesTitle": "The 27 message types",
  "docMessagesIntro": "Every AIS payload begins with a 6-bit message type (1 to 27). The catalog below groups them by family. Each card shows a real NMEA sentence generated by KikAis' own encoder, its decoded fields, and a button to open it in the Decoder.",

  "docNmeaTitle": "NMEA & AIVDM framing",
  "docNmeaIntro": "On the wire, AIS messages travel as NMEA 0183 sentences starting with !AIVDM (other ships) or !AIVDO (your own ship). The payload is an ASCII-armored bit vector.",
  "docNmeaSampleSingle": "!AIVDM,1,1,,B,177KQJ5000G?tO`K>RA1wUbN0TKH,0*5C",
  "docNmeaFields": "Sentence fields",
  "docNmeaField1": "Talker & formatter — !AIVDM or !AIVDO (see talker IDs below).",
  "docNmeaField2": "Fragment count — how many sentences make up the full message (NMEA limits each line to ~82 characters).",
  "docNmeaField3": "Fragment number — which part this is (1-based).",
  "docNmeaField4": "Sequential message ID — ties fragments of the same message together.",
  "docNmeaField5": "Radio channel — A or B (AIS1 / AIS2).",
  "docNmeaField6": "Data payload — the six-bit armoured AIS payload.",
  "docNmeaField7": "Fill bits — how many pad bits were added to the last 6-bit group (0-5).",
  "docNmeaField8": "Checksum — the XOR of all bytes before the *, in hexadecimal.",
  "docNmeaMulti": "Multi-fragment messages",
  "docNmeaMultiText": "Messages longer than one line (such as type 5 static data) are split: the first sentence reports a fragment count of 2 and the second completes it with the same message ID.",
  "docNmeaSampleMulti": "!AIVDM,2,1,3,B,55P5TL01VIaAL@7WKO@mBplU@<PDhh000000001S;AJ::4A80?4i@E53,0*3E\n!AIVDM,2,2,3,B,1@0000000000000,2*55",
  "docNmeaArmoring": "Six-bit armoring",
  "docNmeaArmoringText": "Each payload character holds 6 bits. Subtract 48 from the ASCII code, then subtract another 8 if the result is above 40.",
  "docNmeaTalkers": "Talker IDs",
  "docNmeaTalkersIntro": "Different NMEA 4.0 talker IDs identify the type of AIS station:",
  "docTalkerRow": "!{talker} — {label}",
  "docNmeaChecksum": "Checksum",
  "docNmeaChecksumText": "The trailing checksum is the XOR of every byte between the \"!\" and the \"*\". Calculate your own below:",
  "docNmeaInspectorTitle": "Try it: sentence inspector",
  "docNmeaInspectorText": "Paste any AIVDM/AIVDO sentence (or use a sample above) to see its fields broken down and the decoded values.",

  "docPayloadIntro": "Once the six-bit armoring is undone, an AIS payload is a sequence of bit fields. The first six bits are the message type; the next two are the repeat indicator; then come 30 bits of MMSI.",
  "docPayloadCnb": "The Common Navigation Block (types 1-3)",
  "docPayloadCnbText": "The most important layout is shared by the Class A position reports. Use the selector to browse the main message layouts, and click a segment to read what it encodes.",
  "docPayloadCoords": "Coordinates",
  "docPayloadCoordsText": "Latitude and longitude are stored in 1/10 000 of a minute. Divide by 600 000 to get degrees: 60 minutes in a degree, and 10 000 units per minute. East/North are positive.",
  "docPayloadCoordsCode": "lon = rawLongitude / 600000.0   // e.g. -26940000 -> -44.9°",
  "docPayloadCoordsConvert": "Convert your own coordinates below:",
  "docPayloadSpeed": "Speed, course, heading",
  "docPayloadSpeed1": "SOG — speed over ground in tenths of a knot (0-102.2 kn); 1023 means \"not available\".",
  "docPayloadSpeed2": "COG — course over ground in tenths of a degree, relative to true north.",
  "docPayloadSpeed3": "Heading — true heading in whole degrees; 511 means \"not available\".",
  "docPayloadSpeed4": "ROT — rate of turn: value ≈ 4.733 × √(turning rate in °/min), signed (positive = right).",
  "docPayloadNavStatus": "Navigation status",
  "docPayloadEpfd": "Position fix type (EPFD)",
  "docPayloadText": "Six-bit text",
  "docPayloadTextIntro": "Names, call signs and destinations use the same six-bit alphabet as the payload itself. Lowercase letters cannot be encoded, which is why AIS names are usually uppercase.",

  "docSecurityTitle": "Security & data quality",
  "docSecurityIntro": "AIS is designed for cooperation, not security. The radio channel is open and unencrypted, and there is no authentication of who is broadcasting.",
  "docSecurityThreats": "Threats",
  "docSecurityThreat1": "Spoofing — transmitting a fake MMSI, position or identity (phantom ships, sanctions evasion).",
  "docSecurityThreat2": "Jamming — flooding the two VHF channels so real traffic cannot be received.",
  "docSecurityThreat3": "Meaconing — replaying real signals from elsewhere to confuse receivers.",
  "docSecurityQuality": "Data quality",
  "docSecurityQuality1": "The position accuracy bit distinguishes an unaugmented GNSS fix (> 10 m) from a DGPS-quality fix (< 10 m).",
  "docSecurityQuality2": "Receivers should sanity-check positions, speeds and timestamps; about 0.3% of real-world messages have a bad payload length.",
  "docSecurityQuality3": "Satellite AIS occasionally suffers collisions because the satellite footprint is much larger than a TDMA cell — one more reason to correlate with radar and other sources.",

  "docKikaisIntro": "KikAis is a full AIS lab: receive live or simulated traffic, decode it, inspect and send your own messages, and build fleets. Here is how each tab maps to what you just read.",
  "docTabReceptionText": "Choose feeds (file, serial, simulation), start the forwarder and watch the raw NMEA stream and the decoded boats.",
  "docTabSendText": "Forward the received sentences to one or more TCP/UDP targets — how a shore station would distribute traffic.",
  "docTabMapText": "See decoded vessels plotted from their type 1/2/3, 18, 19 and 27 position reports.",
  "docTabEditorText": "Build any of the 27 message types by hand from a friendly form and send it — the best way to learn the fields.",
  "docTabDecoderText": "Paste any sentence and get the decoded fields, checksum and fragment handling — the practical companion to this guide.",
  "docTabStatsText": "Message counters, rates per feed and decoder health (invalid checksums, dropped fragments).",
